    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The use of pulse testing as a means of evaluating reservoirs is fairly recent. Like the pioneer work of Johnson, et al, most of the known research on pulse testing has concentrated on the use of horizontal pulse testing for evaluating reservoir transmissibility and storage. There is no known study on vertical multi-pulse testing in thick formations even though such tests offer good potential for yielding the formation's vertical and horizontal permeabilities. The purpose of this research therefore is to study the dynamics of vertical multi-pulse testing in a radially infinite slab reservoir, and to generate correlation curves for interpreting pulse test data for various test configurations. The general describing equation for the system's dynamics was formulated by developing the Green's function for a finite solid cylindrical source system. This was degenerated to an equivalent point source solution to obtain the system's describing memory function. The pulse response due to a periodic pulse operation of the reservoir system was obtained by the application of superposition in time on the system's memory function. Using the system's multi-pulse response and the steady periodic response, expressed in terms of Fourier series, a generalized computer program was written to evaluate the system's dimensionless pulse response amplitudes and the dimensionless time-lags. The gradient method of Johnson, et al, was the basic format for the evaluation of these pulse response characteristics. The pulse response characteristics obtained from the computer analysis were transformed such that response profiles for all test configurations could be presented on a single, semi-infinite-acting response profile. This, considered for a wide range of pulse durations, forms the basis for the construction of correlation curves necessary for interpreting pulse test data. Pulse test data analysis procedures were formulated for cases where the formation thickness is either known or unknown. In the former case, an analysis scheme similar to that developed by Brigham was developed for the infinite-acting test configurations. The semi-infinite and the finite-acting test configurations were analysed using an iterative scheme which assumes the infinite-acting scheme as the initial stage of analysis. Approximate analysis schemes were developed for cases where formation thicknesses are unknown. It was found that, for all practical purposes, the system's dynamics can be modelled on the basis of the point source solution. Also, by the application of the reciprocity principle, it was found that analysis on only 50% of the system's response range is sufficient to describe the entire system. The other half is an exact duplicate of this.",
